linked_list(ring, clockwise):
```rust
fn linked_list(&mut self, points: &[Point], clockwise: bool) -> Option<usize> {
    let len = points.len();
    let mut sum = 0.0f64;
    let mut j = if len > 0 { len - 1 } else { 0 };
    for i in 0..len {
        let p1 = points[i];
        let p2 = points[j];
        sum += (p2[0] - p1[0]) * (p1[1] + p2[1]);
        j = i;
    }
    let mut last = NULL;
    if clockwise == (sum > 0.0) {
        for i in 0..len { last = self.insert_node(i as u32, points[i][0], points[i][1], last); }
    } else {
        let mut i = len;
        while i > 0 { i -= 1; last = self.insert_node(i as u32, points[i][0], points[i][1], last); }
    }
    if last != NULL && self.equals(last, self.nodes[last].next) {
        self.remove_node(last);
        last = self.nodes[last].next;
    }
    if last == NULL { None } else { Some(last) }
}
```

Careful: remove_node(last) unlinks; last->next after removal — in C++, `last = last->next;` — but remove_node modifies last->prev->next and last->next->prev, not last's own pointers. So last->next still valid. In Rust arena, fine.

